From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:43:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: honor SEND_AFTER_DTIM for bmc frames on SDIO and
 PCIe

rtw_tx_queue_mapping() puts every broadcast and multicast data frame
into RTW_TX_QUEUE_HI0. The SDIO and PCIe paths use this function. The
high queue is the after DTIM queue of the chip. In AP mode the hardware
holds these frames and sends them only right after a DTIM beacon,
inside the ATIM window. The default window of 2 TU drains about three
frames per DTIM.

While the frames wait they occupy pages of the shared TX page pool. On
RTL8822CS the high queue owns 64 pages and then takes from the 1681
public pages. Any sustained broadcast or multicast rate above the drain
rate fills the pool. The beacon and reserved page download and the H2C
queue draw from the same pool. Once the pool is empty the SDIO free
page check rejects them with EBUSY. The driver then logs "failed to
write data to rsvd page", "failed to download drv rsvd page" and
"failed to download beacon" on every TIM update. The data queues lose
their share of the public pages as well and the link stalls. The SDIO
TX worker retries a starved queue a thousand times with three register
reads per attempt before it moves on. The stall therefore also shows up
as a burst of SDIO commands and long ping times.

mac80211 already decides which frames need after DTIM delivery. It sets
IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM on a broadcast or multicast frame only
while at least one station is dozing. Map only those frames to the high
queue. Everything else leaves on its access category queue at line
rate. This is the SDIO and PCIe counterpart of
commit c710c7c2e038 ("wifi: rtw88: usb: route bmc frames via the high
queue only for DTIM delivery"). That commit made the same change in the
USB queue mapping. The vendor driver behaves the same way. It only
selects the high queue for frames it has buffered for a sleeping
station.

Observed on an RTL8822CS in a NanoPi R2S Plus running as an AP with one
client. Before this change the reserved page download failed every few
seconds, ping times reached 770 ms and TCP connections timed out.
With this change the same setup shows no page starvation and no
reserved page failure. Ping times are 2 to 3 ms with the client awake
and TCP throughput is about 60 Mbit/s in each direction, also during a
10 minute soak with the client in power save. A dozing client together
with about 250 group frames per second still fills the pool. That case
needs the budget and the filter that the USB path already has.
STA mode was not affected because a station never addresses data frames
to a group address. The PCIe path shares this mapping and was not tested
by the author, so Realtek may want to confirm the change on a PCIe part.

The analysis of the stall and the first draft of this patch and its
changelog were produced in Claude Code sessions with the Claude model
claude-fable-5-1. The same sessions ran the tests on an RTL8822CS in a
NanoPi R2S Plus. The author reviewed the change and the results.

Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/tx.c | 6 ++++--
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/tx.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/tx.c
@@ -725,6 +725,7 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL(rtw_tx_ac_to_hwq);
 enum rtw_tx_queue_type rtw_tx_queue_mapping(struct sk_buff *skb)
 {
 	struct ieee80211_hdr *hdr = (struct ieee80211_hdr *)skb->data;
+	struct ieee80211_tx_info *info = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb);
 	__le16 fc = hdr->frame_control;
 	u8 q_mapping = skb_get_queue_mapping(skb);
 	enum rtw_tx_queue_type queue;
@@ -733,8 +734,9 @@ enum rtw_tx_queue_type rtw_tx_queue_mapp
 		queue = RTW_TX_QUEUE_BCN;
 	else if (unlikely(ieee80211_is_mgmt(fc) || ieee80211_is_ctl(fc)))
 		queue = RTW_TX_QUEUE_MGMT;
-	else if (is_broadcast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1) ||
-		 is_multicast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1))
+	else if ((is_broadcast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1) ||
+		  is_multicast_ether_addr(hdr->addr1)) &&
+		 (info->flags & IEEE80211_TX_CTL_SEND_AFTER_DTIM))
 		queue = RTW_TX_QUEUE_HI0;
 	else if (WARN_ON_ONCE(q_mapping >= ARRAY_SIZE(ac_to_hwq)))
 		queue = ac_to_hwq[IEEE80211_AC_BE];
